   "line": [
    "Qb6"
   ],
   "spoken": "One appeal of the c5 break is that after c3 the queen swings to b6 and the first player is already close to worse — starved for a good move, with the b-pawn under fire. This is a signature London theme: with the queen on b6, a bishop to f5 exploits the loose queenside, forking heavy pieces, and a follow-up knight to d3 uses the pin to make the piece untouchable and drags the king into the open.",
   "kind": "tactic"
  },
  {
   "ply": 7,
   "t": 2565.5,
   "fen": "rnb1kb1r/pp1ppppp/1q3n2/2p5/3P1B2/2P5/PP1NPPPP/R2QKBNR b KQkq - 2 4",
   "line": [
    "Nd2"
   ],
   "spoken": "The better, lesser-known try is a knight to d2 — grabbing the b-pawn leads to insane complications, so the clean path is to just plant d5 and reach a superior version of a standard London.",
   "kind": "plan"
  },
