   "line": [
    "Na3"
   ],
   "spoken": "The engine's one bid for an edge is the very rare knight to a3 rather than c3 — and the whole difference is that it leaves the c-pawn free to advance to c3 and shore up the d-pawn. When you play the white side of a London, keeping that d4-pawn well guarded is the essence of the system.",
   "kind": "plan",
   "reanchor": true
  },
  {
   "ply": 10,
   "t": 3252.5,
   "fen": "rnb1kb1r/pp1ppppp/1q6/3n4/3P1B2/N7/PPP2PPP/R2QKBNR w KQkq - 3 6",
   "line": [
    "Nd5"
   ],
   "spoken": "The interesting reply is a knight hop to d5, forcing the bishop to shuffle all the way home to c1 — and a natural-looking knight to c4 walks into a queen check on e6 that hits bishop and king at once. Active piece play breeds these tactics.",
   "kind": "tactic",
   "reanchor": true
  },
